The Security Council,
Recalling its resolutions 2014 (2011), 2051 (2012), 2140 (2014), 2201 (2015), 2216 (2015), 2266 (2016) and 2342 (2017), and its relevant presidential statements,
Having considered the report of the Panel of Experts on Yemen submitted pursuant to resolution 2342 (2017), contained in document S/2018/68,
Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of Yemen,
Reaffirming the need for all parties to comply with their obligations under international humanitarian law and international human rights law, including the obligation to respect and protect civilians and civilian objects,
Expressing grave concern at the continued deterioration of the humanitarian situation in Yemen, including the risk of famine, widespread food insecurity, cholera, displacement and the serious impact on civilians, and stressing the need for rapid, safe and unimpeded humanitarian access to all affected areas,
Expressing grave concern at reports of violations and abuses of human rights and international humanitarian law, including attacks on civilians and civilian infrastructure, obstruction of humanitarian assistance, the recruitment and use of children, sexual and gender-based violence, and attacks against United Nations personnel and humanitarian workers,
Condemning in the strongest terms attacks by Houthi forces against Saudi Arabia, including missile attacks, and attacks on vessels, and condemning all acts of violence and all violations of international humanitarian law,
Recalling the arms embargo imposed by paragraph 14 of resolution 2216 (2015), expressing serious concern at continued violations of the arms embargo and at the findings of the Panel of Experts, and stressing the need for all Member States to implement the embargo fully and effectively,
Welcoming the appointment of Martin Griffiths as Special Envoy of the Secretary-General for Yemen and expressing full support for his efforts to resume an inclusive political process,
Reaffirming its commitment to the political transition in Yemen based on the Gulf Cooperation Council initiative, the outcomes of the National Dialogue Conference and the implementation of resolution 2216 (2015),
Determining that the situation in Yemen constitutes a threat to international peace and security,
Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Reaffirms its support for a Yemeni-led and Yemeni-owned political process and calls upon all parties to engage constructively, in good faith and without preconditions with the Special Envoy of the Secretary-General for Yemen towards a comprehensive political settlement;

2. Reaffirms the importance of the full implementation of resolution 2216 (2015) and calls upon all parties to comply with their obligations under relevant Security Council resolutions, to cease hostilities and to take confidence-building measures, including the release of prisoners and detainees;

3. Urges all parties to facilitate rapid, safe, unhindered and sustained humanitarian access to all affected areas, to protect humanitarian personnel, facilities and assets, and to ensure the delivery of essential goods and services to the civilian population, and calls upon Member States to contribute to the United Nations humanitarian appeal for Yemen;

4. Condemns in the strongest terms attacks by Houthi forces against Saudi Arabia, including missile attacks, and attacks on vessels, demands that such attacks cease immediately, and calls upon all parties to respect their obligations under international humanitarian law and to refrain from attacks against civilians and civilian objects;

5. Reiterates the obligation of all Member States to implement fully the arms embargo imposed by paragraph 14 of resolution 2216 (2015), calls upon all Member States to take the necessary measures to prevent the direct or indirect supply, sale or transfer of weapons and military equipment to the Houthis, and calls upon all States to report to the Panel of Experts violations of the arms embargo and other acts that threaten the peace, security and stability of Yemen;

6. Reaffirms the measures imposed by paragraphs 11 and 15 of resolution 2140 (2014) and the arms embargo imposed by paragraph 14 of resolution 2216 (2015), and expresses its intention to consider additional designations, as appropriate, including of individuals or entities obstructing humanitarian access or violating the arms embargo;

7. Decides to renew until 26 February 2019 the measures set out in paragraphs 11 and 15 of resolution 2140 (2014) and paragraph 14 of resolution 2216 (2015), as renewed by resolution 2342 (2017);

8. Decides to extend the mandate of the Panel of Experts, as set out in resolution 2140 (2014) and extended by resolution 2342 (2017), until 28 March 2019;

9. Requests the Panel of Experts to submit a midterm report to the Security Council, through the Committee, no later than 25 July 2018 and a final report no later than 25 January 2019, and further requests the Panel to brief the Security Council upon the submission of the final report;

10. Requests all parties and all Member States, as well as international, regional and subregional organizations, to cooperate fully with the Panel of Experts and to ensure the safety and unhindered access of its members, and calls upon all parties to provide the Panel with the information it requires to fulfil its mandate;

11. Decides to remain seized of the matter.

